## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates the ability to design realistic cross-service end-to-end tests with real-time updates, failure modes, and proper data isolation.

## Key Concepts
- Real-time event streams (WebSocket/SSE) and state machine validation
- Test isolation with per-tenant data and fresh environments
- Deterministic inputs via seed data and mocked external services
- Idempotency and eventual consistency verification across services
- Failure injection testing (disconnects, latency, partial outages)

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-test sketch using Playwright
const { test, expect } = require('@playwright/test');

test.describe('E2E Food Delivery Flow', () => {
  let wsServer, testData;

  test.beforeAll(async () => {
    wsServer = await startMockWebSocketServer();
    testData = await seedDeterministicTestData();
  });

  test('complete delivery flow with driver tracking', async ({ page }) => {
    const driverUpdates = [];

    // Simulate real-time driver location stream
    wsServer.on('driver-location', (data) => driverUpdates.push(data));

    // 1. Place order
    await page.goto('/order');
    await page.fill('#address', testData.address);
    await page.click('#place-order');
    const orderId = await expectOrderConfirmed(page);

    // 2. Restaurant accepts
    await wsServer.emit('order-status', { orderId, status: 'accepted' });
    await expectOrderStatus(page, 'accepted');

    // 3. Driver assigned
    await wsServer.emit('driver-assigned', { orderId, driver: testData.driver });
    await expectDriverAssigned(page, testData.driver);

    // 4. Verify location/ETA streaming
    await wsServer.emit('driver-location', { lat: 40.7128, lng: -74.0060, eta: 10 });
    await expectETA(page, '10 min');

    // 5. Payment processed
    await stubPaymentAPI({ orderId, status: 'success' });
    await expectPaymentStatus(page, 'paid');

    // 6. Delivery completes
    await wsServer.emit('order-status', { orderId, status: 'delivered' });
    await expectOrderStatus(page, 'delivered');

    // Verify idempotent event handling
    await wsServer.emit('order-status', { orderId, status: 'delivered' });
    expect(driverUpdates).toHaveLength(1); // No duplicate processing

    // Verify eventual consistency
    await expectExternalServiceSynced(orderId);
  });

  test('handles driver disconnect gracefully', async ({ page }) => {
    const orderId = await placeOrder(page);
    await wsServer.simulateDisconnect();

    await expectReconnectionLogic(page);
    await expectDriverReassigned(page);
  });
});
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests for offline-first flows are common in modern web apps and require practical use of network emulation, event queues, and deterministic conflict handling. This question probes hands-on ability to design reliable E2E tests that stay robust under connectivity changes.

## Key Concepts

- Offline-first UX and service workers
- Network emulation in Playwright or Cypress
- Backend API mocking and idempotent sync
- Deterministic conflict resolution policy
- Data seeding for repeatable tests

## Code Example

```javascript
// Playwright test skeleton
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

test('offline create and sync with deterministic merge', async ({ browser }) => {
  const context = await browser.newContext();
  const page = await context.newPage();
  // seed data via API (omitted for brevity)
  // go offline
  await context.setOffline(true);
  await page.goto('https://app.example');
  await page.fill('#title', 'Offline note');
  await page.fill('#body', 'content');
  await page.click('#save');
  await expect(page.locator('#status')).toHaveText('Queued');
  // go online and sync
  await context.setOffline(false);
  const resp = await page.waitForResponse('/api/notes');
  // assume server returns created note with serverId
  await expect(resp.status()).toBe(201);
  // verify note appears and is synced
  await expect(page.locator('.note-title').last()).toHaveText('Offline note');
  // optional conflict scenario in a second tab would be tested similarly
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this test for multiple concurrent offline edits across tabs?
- How would you ensure test reliability when the backend response latency varies?

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates the candidate's understanding of end-to-end testing for real-time, distributed systems with regional routing, data consistency, and feature flag safety.

## Key Concepts
- End-to-end testing of event-driven pipelines, cross-region latency, and eventual consistency
- Test data isolation and deterministic environments via mocks and seed data
- Idempotency, replay safety, and rollback semantics for feature flags
- Instrumentation hooks to seed Kafka, mock external feeds, and reset Redis

## Code Example
```javascript
// Playwright test scaffold for the trading alert pipeline
const { test, expect } = require('@playwright/test');

test.describe('Multi-Region Alerting System', () => {
  let kafkaSeeder;
  let redisClient;
  let mockExternalFeed;

  test.beforeEach(async () => {
    // Seed isolated test data per region
    await redisClient.flushdb();
    await kafkaSeeder.seedEvents([
      { region: 'us-east', symbol: 'AAPL', threshold: 150 },
      { region: 'eu-west', symbol: 'BMW', threshold: 85 },
    ]);
    mockExternalFeed.setDeterministicResponse({ AAPL: { price: 152 }, BMW: { price: 87 } });
  });

  test('alerts propagate to correct regional dashboards', async ({ page }) => {
    await page.goto('https://dashboard.trading.example.com');
    await expect(page.locator('.alert').first()).toBeVisible({ timeout: 5000 });

    // Verify cross-region routing
    const alerts = await page.locator('.alert').all();
    expect(alerts).toHaveLength(2);
  });

  test('feature flag rollback triggers correctly', async ({ page }) => {
    await page.goto('https://dashboard.trading.example.com');
    await page.evaluate(() => window.triggerRollback());
    await expect(page.locator('.rollback-success')).toBeVisible();
  });
});
```

